# prompts/conformance/directives_python.prompt
% You are an expert Python engineer. Your goal is to write the BREAKING-CHANGE
% directive parser and environment-flag helpers in `pdd/conformance/directives.py`.

% Role & Scope
  The public-surface and test-churn gates block generation by default. A prompt
  author opts out of a SPECIFIC blocked change by writing an anchored
  `BREAKING-CHANGE:` line in the prompt BODY. This module owns that grammar, so
  both gates agree on exactly which directives count. It contains no gate logic
  and raises nothing.

<include>context/python_preamble.prompt</include>

% Requirements

  1. **`_parse_llm_bool(value: str) -> bool`** - parse an LLM-enable flag.
     An empty/falsy input returns `True` (LLM enabled by default). Otherwise
     lowercase and strip: `0`, `false`, `no`, `off` return `False`; only
     `1`, `true`, `yes`, `on` return `True`; anything else returns `False`.

  2. **`_env_flag_enabled(name: str) -> bool`** - return `True` only when the env
     var is present AND its stripped lowercase value is one of `1`, `true`,
     `yes`, `on`. An unset variable returns `False`.

  3. **`_strip_yaml_front_matter(prompt_content: Optional[str]) -> str`** -
     opt-outs must come from the prompt BODY, never from metadata, so an indented
     directive inside front matter cannot whitelist a removal. This is the
     stripped form every parser below must see.
     - The block begins with `---` on line 1 (after an optional UTF-8 BOM) and
       closes with a `---` line. Accept CRLF and mixed line endings, trailing
       whitespace on either fence, and a closing fence that is the final line
       with no trailing newline.
     - An UNTERMINATED opening fence is left alone - never silently swallow the
       whole prompt body.
     - A leading UTF-8 BOM with no front matter is still stripped on its own, so
       a BOM-only prompt does not retain it ahead of the first directive line.
     - `None`/empty input returns `""`.

  4. **`_prompt_has_breaking_change_marker(prompt_content) -> bool`** - `True`
     when the front-matter-stripped body contains the substring
     `BREAKING-CHANGE:` anywhere. `_prompt_allows_breaking_change` is a
     backward-compatible wrapper that simply delegates to it.

  5. **`_iter_breaking_change_directives(prompt_content) -> List[str]`** - return
     the directive TAIL of each anchored directive line, stripped.
     - **Anchored** means the first non-whitespace tokens on the line are
       `BREAKING-CHANGE:`. Match with `re.MULTILINE` on a pattern equivalent to
       `^[ \t]*BREAKING-CHANGE:[ \t]*(?P<directive>.*)$`.
     - This is what makes buried mid-line mentions inert. A documentation line
       such as `Use BREAKING-CHANGE: remove old_helper to opt out` MUST NOT
       register as a real directive.
     - Scan the front-matter-stripped body only.

  6. **`_parse_breaking_change_symbols(directive_tail: str) -> Set[str]`** -
     parse the comma-separated symbol list that follows an action verb.
     - Drop a single trailing sentence terminator (`.`, `;`, `:`) so
       `remove old_helper.` parses cleanly.
     - Split on `,`; each piece must match, in full, an optionally-wrapped dotted
       identifier: optional leading whitespace, an optional wrapper character
       (backtick, single quote or double quote), a dotted Python identifier
       (`[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*` joined by `.`), the SAME wrapper again if one
       was opened, then optional trailing whitespace and end of piece.
     - Wrappers MUST match on both sides - use a backreference/conditional so
       `"old_helper'` is rejected.
     - Pieces that do not match in full are silently skipped. Because a token
       containing embedded whitespace can never match, prose is rejected:
       `BREAKING-CHANGE: remove old_helper to opt out` whitelists ONLY
       `old_helper`, never `to`/`opt`/`out`. For multi-symbol opt-outs the author
       must omit trailing prose entirely.

  7. **`_prompt_breaking_change_removed_symbols(prompt_content) -> Set[str]`** -
     the removal allow-list. For each anchored directive, the tail must BEGIN
     with a removal verb, matched case-insensitively and anchored at the start:
     `remove`/`removes`/`removed`/`removing`, `delete`/`deletes`/`deleted`/`deleting`,
     `drop`/`drops`/`dropped`/`dropping`, `rename`/`renames`/`renamed`/`renaming`.
     Directives that do not start with one of these are skipped. Parse the
     remainder with `_parse_breaking_change_symbols` and union the results.

  8. **`_prompt_breaking_change_signature_symbols(prompt_content) -> Set[str]`** -
     the signature-change allow-list. Same anchored-directive scan, but the tail
     must begin with a `change` verb (`change`/`changes`/`changed`/`changing`)
     followed by whitespace and one of `signature`/`signatures`/`api`/`contract`.
     Parse the remainder identically.

  9. **A bare `BREAKING-CHANGE:` marker MUST NOT disable a gate globally.** With
     no verb and no symbols, both allow-lists come back empty and both gates stay
     armed. Opt-outs are always scoped to named symbols.

% Notes for the callers (not implemented here)
  - Descendant expansion - a top-level class name in the REMOVAL allow-list
    implicitly authorizes removing its captured `Class.method` /
    `Class.Inner.method` descendants - is applied by the public-surface gate, not
    by this module. It is deliberately removal-only: `change signature Service`
    is ambiguous (constructor? every method?) and silently broadening it would
    mask real regressions.
  - The test-churn gate has its OWN verb-object grammar (an opt-out verb whose
    direct object is `tests`) and lives in `test_churn.py`.

% Dependencies
  - Imports `os` and `re`.
  - Imports `List`, `Optional`, `Set` from `typing`.

% Instructions
  - Compile the two regexes once at module level.
  - Every parser must tolerate `None` and empty input by returning an empty
    result rather than raising.

% Deliverables
  - Code: `pdd/conformance/directives.py`
